<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:fluent="using:Avalonia.Themes.Fluent">
  <!-- ==========================================================
       A design theme: one complete look, palette and geometry and
       type together, swapped as a unit at run time.

       It is a Styles rather than a ResourceDictionary because a look
       is not only colours. The corner radii, the caption face and
       the pressed-button behaviour are as much a part of a theme as
       the palette is, and Fluent's own palette — which paints every
       stock control — can only be set from inside a Styles. Putting
       all of it in one file means a theme cannot be half-applied.

       Each theme file must define the same set of App* resource keys
       in both a Light and a Dark dictionary; the views bind to those
       keys and never to a colour.
       ========================================================== -->

  <Styles.Resources>
    <ResourceDictionary>
      <!-- ============================================================
           Cyrk 66 — the design system this application is dressed in.
           Sampled from Danuta Zukowska's 1966 Polish circus poster: a
           night-blue ground, one hot pink accent, paper-white neutrals
           and ink-black rules. Three things carry the identity, and
           they are the three things a stock desktop app never does:

             1. No corners. The poster is a printed sheet; everything
                here is a rectangle. Radii are zero, not "small".
             2. Ink borders and a hard offset shadow on anything you
                can press. Not a blur — a second rectangle, printed
                slightly off-register, the way two-colour litho misses.
             3. Captions are uppercase and widely tracked. The labels
                read as catalogue numbers on a playbill rather than as
                form furniture. The kit sets them in a mono as well;
                see the note on the font resources for why that part
                does not survive the move to a desktop.

           Night is the design system's default and this app's dark
           variant; the light variant is the paper stock the poster was
           printed on, for the same reason the kit ships one — dense
           forms and tables want ink on paper, not glow on night.
           ============================================================ -->

      <!-- One embedded face, for the wordmark, and the platform's own for
           everything else. Files live in Assets/Fonts (SIL OFL).

           The text face used to be Space Grotesk, embedded on the argument
           that a design system falling back to Arial is not applied at
           all. That argument holds for a display face seen once at 44px
           and does not hold for the body of a desktop application. Two
           measurements settled it: Space Grotesk's x-height is 0.486 of
           its em against the macOS system face's 0.508, so at any given
           size its lowercase renders about four percent smaller — small
           enough to look like a mistake rather than a choice, and a real
           part of why the captions read as undersized. Its cap-height,
           meanwhile, is 0.700 against 0.705, so the uppercase tracked
           captions that carry most of this theme's voice look the same
           either way. The identity was riding on the half of the face
           this theme barely uses.

           A system UI face is also hinted and optically sized for screen
           text at 12px, which a display-leaning grotesque is not, and it
           makes the two themes agree about what body text is — the point
           of a theme here is palette and geometry, not a second opinion
           on legibility.

           What survives is what a display face is for: Bevan, italic, on
           the wordmark alone, where the poster's voice is actually
           audible. See TypographyTests for the one thing this costs — a
           platform face cannot be verified the way an embedded one can.

           FontMono is a key rather than a face for the same reason it
           always was: the lists of file paths in the error banners are
           the one place fixed width earns its keep, and the platform's
           own mono is the right answer there. -->
      <FontFamily x:Key="FontDisplay">avares://MightDo.App/Assets/Fonts#Bevan</FontFamily>
      <FontFamily x:Key="FontText">$Default</FontFamily>
      <FontFamily x:Key="FontMono">Menlo, Consolas, monospace</FontFamily>

      <!-- Square by decree. Fluent reads these two for every stock
           control it templates, so overriding them here squares the
           whole application in one place rather than control by
           control; the explicit setters further down are the backstop
           for the parts that hard-code a radius instead. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="ControlCornerRadius">0</CornerRadius>
      <CornerRadius x:Key="OverlayCornerRadius">0</CornerRadius>

      <!-- The offset ink shadow, once, so pressed states can drop it and
           everything that carries it agrees on the register. -->
      <BoxShadows x:Key="ShadowLetter">3 3 0 0 #2D233A</BoxShadows>

      <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

        <!-- ····· Paper (light) ····· -->
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
          <!-- pink-500 is a highlight on a night ground and turns to
               candyfloss on paper, so the light variant runs the accent
               down to pink-600, which the design system itself uses for
               links and focus once the ground goes light. A Color
               assigned to a Background or a BorderBrush resolves to
               nothing at all — silently, since the binding simply never
               produces a value — so anything painted accent goes through
               the brush, and the Color exists for Fluent alone. -->
          <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColor">#C53798</Color>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppAccentBrush">#C53798</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- Surfaces: playbill stock, warm rather than white. Sunken is
               a level *down* from the window, not up — the poster has no
               elevation, so the kit separates panes by pushing them into
               the sheet and letting the card float back to plain white. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppWindowBrush">#F2F0EA</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppSurfaceBrush">#E5E2D9</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppCardBrush">#FAF9F5</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppChipBrush">#E5E2D9</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppBorderBrush">#CFCBC0</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppCardBorderBrush">#CFCBC0</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- Ink, and ink gone quiet. Both are real colours picked
               against the paper rather than black at reduced opacity: a
               half-transparent black over a tinted chip is a different
               colour from a half-transparent black over the card, and
               only one of them was chosen. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppTextBrush">#2D233A</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppTextSecondaryBrush">#6B6076</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- The ink the poster's rules and button borders are printed
               in. Full-strength, never a tint — the 2px keyline around a
               primary button is the design system's loudest signature. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppInkBrush">#2D233A</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppStrongBorderBrush">#9D95A5</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppHoverBrush">#0D2D233A</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppActiveBrush">#172D233A</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppFocusBrush">#C53798</SolidColorBrush>

          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppDisabledBrush">#E4E4E4</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppDisabledBorderBrush">#D2D2D2</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppDisabledTextBrush">#757575</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- The lettering rose, taken down past rose-600 until it
               clears 4.5:1 on both the window and a card. The design
               system picked its reds against a night ground; on paper
               they have to be darker to say the same thing. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OverdueBrush">#B23652</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- All four priorities, because three of them tinted and one
               of them left plain is not a scale — it is two states and a
               gap, and Medium fell in the gap looking exactly like the
               category chip beside it.

               The sheet runs warm for what is urgent and cool for what is
               not: Critical takes the lettering rose, High the poster's
               amber, Medium the periwinkle the night ground is mixed
               from, Low the same periwinkle gone pale. Temperature says
               whether to care; depth says how much. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityCriticalBackgroundBrush">#F7D8E4</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityCriticalForegroundBrush">#9E2278</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityHighBackgroundBrush">#F6E7BE</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityHighForegroundBrush">#6B4E12</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityMediumBackgroundBrush">#C6D0EE</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityMediumForegroundBrush">#2E3789</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityLowBackgroundBrush">#EAE7F5</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityLowForegroundBrush">#575081</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- Text painted over the raw accent colour. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OnAccentBrush">#FAF9F5</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- Quiet, live, done. The design system's own three status
               colours, with the quiet grey and the mint both darkened
               until an 8px dot still separates from the header behind it —
               a green that belongs to this palette rather than a stock
               one borrowed in for the sake of a traffic light. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusInitialBrush">#7A7183</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusActiveBrush">#C53798</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusFinalBrush">#3F8C71</SolidColorBrush>
        </ResourceDictionary>

        <!-- ····· Night (dark) ····· -->
        <!-- The poster ground, top to bottom: night-950 through
             night-500. Elevation is the ramp itself — panes sink toward
             950, cards sit at 800, chips lift to 700 — because a shadow
             carries almost nothing against a dark ground, and the one
             shadow this system owns is a hard ink offset that would
             vanish into it. -->
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
          <Color x:Key="SystemAccentColor">#FC6BCC</Color>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppAccentBrush">#FC6BCC</SolidColorBrush>

          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppWindowBrush">#22224E</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppSurfaceBrush">#191940</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppCardBrush">#2A2A5E</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppChipBrush">#3B3A6C</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppBorderBrush">#4B4B8D</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppCardBorderBrush">#4B4B8D</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- moon-100, not white. Pure white on a night ground is past
               legible and into glare, and body text is the largest area
               of colour on screen. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppTextBrush">#DEE5E7</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppTextSecondaryBrush">#A9A9CC</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- Ink stays ink in both variants: the keyline and the offset
               shadow are printing, not lighting, and a pink button with
               a pale border is a different design. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppInkBrush">#2D233A</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppStrongBorderBrush">#73DEE5E7</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppHoverBrush">#14DEE5E7</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppActiveBrush">#24DEE5E7</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppFocusBrush">#ECB4EC</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- The one place neutral grey is allowed, in either scheme and
               in either theme. Everything else on screen carries the
               palette, so a control that has gone properly grey reads as
               switched off without a second cue — which only holds while
               nothing else on screen is grey. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppDisabledBrush">#2B2B2B</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppDisabledBorderBrush">#3B3B3B</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AppDisabledTextBrush">#8A8A8A</SolidColorBrush>

          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OverdueBrush">#FF9DAF</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- The cool end has to work harder here, because the ground
               is already indigo: a periwinkle Medium on a night-blue
               sheet is the sheet. So Medium goes to steel and Low to a
               dulled mauve, one hue either side of the ground rather than
               on top of it. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityCriticalBackgroundBrush">#4A2231</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityCriticalForegroundBrush">#FF8FA6</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityHighBackgroundBrush">#3E3720</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityHighForegroundBrush">#F2C14E</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityMediumBackgroundBrush">#123448</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityMediumForegroundBrush">#8FCBEA</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityLowBackgroundBrush">#423C52</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PriorityLowForegroundBrush">#CDC1D4</SolidColorBrush>

          <!-- The night accent is a highlight, so text on it goes to ink. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="OnAccentBrush">#2D233A</SolidColorBrush>

          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusInitialBrush">#7676B0</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusActiveBrush">#FC6BCC</SolidColorBrush>
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StatusFinalBrush">#8FD6C0</SolidColorBrush>
        </ResourceDictionary>
      </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
    </ResourceDictionary>
  </Styles.Resources>

      <!-- Fluent builds every stock control — button fills, field grounds,
           hover states, the accent — from this one palette per variant, and
           it reads it from here rather than from the dictionaries above.
           Leaving it out is how the accent ends up stock Windows blue and
           every button ends up grey.

           Grey is the point of the exercise. Fluent's defaults are pure
           black and pure white at low alpha, and an overlay of neutral over
           a tinted ground averages the tint away. The fills below are opaque
           and carry the palette themselves rather than being mixed from it.

           The exceptions are deliberate: ChromeDisabled* stays flat, so
           switched-off means switched-off and nothing else. -->
      <FluentTheme>
        <FluentTheme.Palettes>
          <fluent:ColorPaletteResources x:Key="Light"
                                        Accent="#C53798"
                                        RegionColor="#F2F0EA"
                                        AltHigh="#FFFFFF"
                                        AltLow="#33FFFFFF"
                                        AltMedium="#99FFFFFF"
                                        AltMediumHigh="#CCFFFFFF"
                                        AltMediumLow="#66FFFFFF"
                                        BaseHigh="#2D233A"
                                        BaseLow="#E5E2D9"
                                        BaseMedium="#992D233A"
                                        BaseMediumHigh="#CC2D233A"
                                        BaseMediumLow="#662D233A"
                                        ChromeAltLow="#2D233A"
                                        ChromeBlackHigh="#2D233A"
                                        ChromeBlackLow="#332D233A"
                                        ChromeBlackMedium="#CC2D233A"
                                        ChromeBlackMediumLow="#662D233A"
                                        ChromeHigh="#CFCBC0"
                                        ChromeLow="#FAF9F5"
                                        ChromeMedium="#E5E2D9"
                                        ChromeMediumLow="#F2F0EA"
                                        ChromeWhite="#FFFFFF"
                                        ListLow="#192D233A"
                                        ListMedium="#332D233A"
                                        ErrorText="#C33F5E" />
          <fluent:ColorPaletteResources x:Key="Dark"
                                        Accent="#FC6BCC"
                                        RegionColor="#22224E"
                                        AltHigh="#22224E"
                                        AltLow="#3322224E"
                                        AltMedium="#9922224E"
                                        AltMediumHigh="#CC22224E"
                                        AltMediumLow="#6622224E"
                                        BaseHigh="#DEE5E7"
                                        BaseLow="#3B3A6C"
                                        BaseMedium="#99DEE5E7"
                                        BaseMediumHigh="#CCDEE5E7"
                                        BaseMediumLow="#66DEE5E7"
                                        ChromeAltLow="#DEE5E7"
                                        ChromeBlackHigh="#191940"
                                        ChromeBlackLow="#33191940"
                                        ChromeBlackMedium="#CC191940"
                                        ChromeBlackMediumLow="#66191940"
                                        ChromeHigh="#4B4B8D"
                                        ChromeLow="#191940"
                                        ChromeMedium="#22224E"
                                        ChromeMediumLow="#2A2A5E"
                                        ChromeWhite="#DEE5E7"
                                        ListLow="#19DEE5E7"
                                        ListMedium="#33DEE5E7"
                                        ErrorText="#E85878" />
        </FluentTheme.Palettes>
      </FluentTheme>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Type. One face, the platform's, doing every job — what
           separates the furniture from the content is size, weight,
           tracking, case and colour rather than a second family. The
           design system splits those roles across two faces, and this
           is the same split made without the second file: the caption
           roles are small, uppercase, tracked and secondary, which is
           four signals of not-body-text before a typeface is asked to
           be a fifth. It still stops a dense form from
           looking like a form: the furniture recedes into catalogue
           type and the content stays plain.

           FontFamily inherits down the visual tree, so the two window
           selectors reach every control including the ones in popups,
           which hang off the window's own overlay layer.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Style Selector="Window">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="UserControl">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- The three caption roles, all mono, all tracked wide enough to
           read as printing rather than as small text. Uppercase is set
           on the strings themselves — Avalonia has no text-transform,
           and a converter on every label would be a lot of machinery
           for eight words that never change. -->
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.section">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="1.6" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,18,0,6" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.group">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="1.5" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextSecondaryBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,5" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.label">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextSecondaryBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="1.5" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,10,0,3" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.hint">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextSecondaryBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Interactive labels: the words on something you press that is
           not a stock button — the view switcher, a radio in a strip of
           chrome. These are not captions. A caption states something and
           is meant to recede; a control label is a target, sits beside
           real buttons, and has to look like it belongs to them. They
           take the toolbar's size, weight and tracking so a row of mixed
           chrome reads as one row.

           They also take the primary ink rather than the secondary: a
           control you can press should not be quieter than the count
           printed next to it. -->
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.control">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.85" />
      </Style>

      <!-- The one place the display face appears in the application
           chrome: the wordmark. Bevan is a poster slab and is unreadable
           below about 20px, so it is never allowed near a control. -->
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.wordmark">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontDisplay}" />
        <Setter Property="FontStyle" Value="Italic" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="44" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Counts, sizes, timestamps and the status line. UI faces set
           proportional figures by default, so a count reflows every time
           it changes and shoves its neighbours along with it. tnum is the
           face's own tabular set, which is all a mono was being asked for
           here — honoured by the macOS system face and by Segoe UI, and
           ignored without complaint by anything that has no OpenType
           features to offer. -->
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.meta">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFeatures" Value="tnum" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.6" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- A due date is quiet until it is overdue, then it is the loudest
           thing on the row. -->
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.due">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFeatures" Value="tnum" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.4" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextSecondaryBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="TextBlock.due.overdue">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource OverdueBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
      </Style>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Geometry. Fluent hard-codes a radius into several templates
           rather than reading ControlCornerRadius, so the square corners
           are asserted again here on the controls this app actually
           uses. Cheap, and it is the single most visible thing about
           this design system.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Style Selector=":is(TemplatedControl)">
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border">
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
      </Style>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Buttons. The default is the design system's secondary: a
           hairline keyline, no fill, body text. Only the accent class
           gets the full poster treatment — pink plate, 2px ink keyline,
           and the hard offset shadow that snaps flat when pressed, so
           the button behaves like a stamp coming down on the page.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Style Selector="Button">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppStrongBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,6" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Button:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppHoverBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppStrongBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Button:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppActiveBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppStrongBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <Style Selector="Button.accent">
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppInkBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource OnAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.8" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Button.accent /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppInkBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource OnAccentBrush}" />
        <!-- The shadow goes on the presenter rather than the button: a
             Button is not a Border and has no BoxShadow of its own. -->
        <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="{StaticResource ShadowLetter}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Button.accent:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppInkBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource OnAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.88" />
      </Style>
      <!-- Pressed drops the shadow and moves the plate onto where the
           shadow was: the whole button travels 3px down and right and
           lands flush with the page. -->
      <Style Selector="Button.accent:pressed">
        <Setter Property="RenderTransform" Value="translate(3px, 3px)" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Button.accent:pressed /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppInkBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource OnAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="none" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Button.accent:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="BoxShadow" Value="none" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Toolbar chrome. Quieter than a button until you are over it —
           the design system's toolbars are borderless until touched, so
           a row of six controls does not read as six boxes.

           The text face, not the caption mono. The kit is specific about
           this and it is worth saying why: mono is for the things the
           application states about itself and never changes — a label, a
           count, a timestamp — where the fixed width is the point. A
           toolbar is words you read and press, six of them in a row, and
           setting those in a mono makes the busiest strip in the window
           the hardest part of it to scan. Medium weight and open tracking
           carry the voice instead. -->
      <Style Selector="Button.toolbar, ToggleButton.toolbar">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextSecondaryBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.85" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,6" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Button.toolbar:pointerover, ToggleButton.toolbar:pointerover">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- The toolbar buttons hold explicit TextBlocks beside their
           icons rather than a bare string, and a TextBlock brings its own
           defaults for anything the theme has not spoken about. This
           reaches them so the face and tracking survive the trip through
           the content template. -->
      <Style Selector="Button.toolbar TextBlock, ToggleButton.toolbar TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource FontText}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.85" />
      </Style>

      <Style Selector="ToggleButton.toolbar:checked /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppActiveBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <Style Selector="ToggleButton">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppStrongBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,6" />
      </Style>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Fields. Sunken rather than raised — the design system puts
           inputs a level *below* the surface they sit on, which is why
           a form here reads as slots cut into the sheet. Focus is the
           accent keyline; there is no glow, because the poster has no
           glow anywhere except the moon.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Style Selector="TextBox /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppSurfaceBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="TextBox:focus /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppFocusBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="ComboBox /template/ Border#Background">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppSurfaceBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Ticked boxes take the accent plate and the ink keyline, the
           same two-colour move as the primary button, one size down. -->
      <Style Selector="CheckBox:checked /template/ Border#NormalRectangle">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppInkBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="CheckBox:checked /template/ Path#CheckGlyph">
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource AppInkBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Chips. Square, hairline, mono. The pill was the one shape the
           poster does not contain, and it was on every chip in the app.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Style Selector="Border.chip">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppChipBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="7,2" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      </Style>
      <!-- Chips carry a status or a category the user named, so they are
           the last place to spend width on a fixed-width face: a board
           column is 260px and "Waiting on someone else" has to fit in it.
           Tabular figures because a chip often is one ("3 of 5"). -->
      <Style Selector="Border.chip > StackPanel > TextBlock, Border.chip > TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFeatures" Value="tnum" />
        <Setter Property="LetterSpacing" Value="0.2" />
      </Style>
      <!-- Every priority tints its chip and takes its own keyline, all
           four built the same way. Medium used to wear no class at all,
           which made it the plain chip - indistinguishable from the
           category beside it - and Low was the plain chip at 70% opacity,
           which is the disabled signal and not a priority at all.

           What separates them now is hue for urgent against calm, depth
           for how far along the scale, and weight as the third cue for
           anyone who cannot use the first two. Bold, bold, medium,
           normal: the ladder is legible in greyscale. -->
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.critical">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityCriticalBackgroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityCriticalForegroundBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.critical > TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityCriticalForegroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.high">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityHighBackgroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityHighForegroundBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.high > TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityHighForegroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.medium">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityMediumBackgroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityMediumForegroundBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.medium > TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityMediumForegroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Medium" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.low">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityLowBackgroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityLowForegroundBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Border.chip.low > TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource PriorityLowForegroundBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- The dot that says what kind of status this is without reading
           it. The one round thing the design system allows, and only
           because it is a badge marker rather than a container. -->
      <Style Selector="Ellipse.statusdot">
        <Setter Property="Width" Value="8" />
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="8" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Ellipse.statusdot.initial">
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource StatusInitialBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Ellipse.statusdot.active">
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource StatusActiveBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="Ellipse.statusdot.final">
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource StatusFinalBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- ============================================================
           List rows. A hairline between them gives the eye somewhere to
           stop; without it a screenful of tasks reads as one block of
           text. The rule goes on the item rather than into the row
           template so the selection highlight sits over it rather than
           under it, and nth-last-child keeps the final row from drawing
           a line into the list's own bottom border.

           Selection lifts the ground rather than filling a bar, so the
           row stays as readable selected as not — see below for why it
           is not the leading accent rule the rest of the system uses.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Style Selector="ListBox.rows > ListBoxItem">
        <!-- The item's own theme sets a foreground, which otherwise wins
             over the window's and leaves the rows on stock black or white
             while everything around them is tinted. -->
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppTextBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,4" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="ListBox.rows > ListBoxItem:not(:nth-last-child(1))">
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,1" />
      </Style>
      <!-- The presenter is also what draws the divider, since Fluent binds
           its border straight from the item's — so selection is expressed in
           the ground rather than in a leading rule. Setting a thickness here
           for a marker would overwrite the row separator, and one of the two
           has to give: a list that has lost its rules is worse than a
           selection that is a shade rather than a stripe. It is also what the
           design system does for table rows, which is what these are. -->
      <Style Selector="ListBox.rows > ListBoxItem /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="ListBox.rows > ListBoxItem:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppHoverBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="ListBox.rows > ListBoxItem:selected /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppActiveBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- ============================================================
           Disabled. Fluent leaves a disabled button the same fill as a
           live one and only fades the label, which reads as "slightly
           quieter" rather than "you cannot press this". These take the
           control all the way to the flat end of the ramp instead.

           The fills have to be set on the template part rather than on
           the control, because Fluent's own disabled styles target those
           parts and would otherwise win.
           ============================================================ -->
      <Style Selector="Button:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledTextBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="ToggleButton:disabled /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBorderBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledTextBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="TextBox:disabled /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="ComboBox:disabled /template/ Border#Background">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="CheckBox:disabled /template/ Border#NormalRectangle">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledBorderBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- The tick is a Path, so it takes a Fill rather than a Foreground,
           and greying the box without it leaves a disabled checkbox with no
           way to say it is ticked. -->
      <Style Selector="CheckBox:disabled /template/ Path#CheckGlyph">
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledTextBrush}" />
      </Style>
      <!-- Text and glyphs, which live outside the parts painted above. -->
      <Style Selector=":is(TemplatedControl):disabled">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppDisabledTextBrush}" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Banners are a slab of the sunken ground with an accent rule
           down the leading edge — the design system's notice, and the
           same marker the selected row uses, so "look here" means one
           thing throughout. -->
      <Style Selector="Border.banner">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource AppSurfaceBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="2,0,0,0" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14,10" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,8" />
      </Style>
</Styles>
